= Discussion and Conclusion

/*
Discuss the results from your evaluation sessions, focusing on the following areas:
• Limitations: Describe any limitations in your system’s design and implementation.
• Lessons Learned: Reflect on what you learned. Explain how you would approach the project differently if you were to start over.
• Future Work: Outline how you would improve the project with additional time. Describe potential future developments.
State the conclusions you can draw from your evaluation sessions.
*/

The project allowed all members of the group to deepen the personal knowledge of Unity and to discover an interesting framework that covers the major aspects of network connectivity: Ubiq.

In particular, the project allowed the team to better understand working approaches when talking about collaborative development of an Unity project using version control systems like Git, a characteristic which is fundamental to learn since, given the internal structure of Unity's project files regarding scene and prefab, teaming up could be a difficult task, especially in case of merge conflicts. The team learn that the beast approach is developing each component separately and as a prefab and possibly test such components in a different scene that should be deleted before merging secondary branches into the main branch of development. A clear separation of task also helps with development.

Unfortunately, it is common opinion that while Ubiq represents certainly an alternative to the native Unity multiplayer system, it is still to be considered under development: due to lack of documentation or implemented functionality, major components like matchmaking and message types exchange had to be implemented manually.

As for the project itself, given a larger amount of development resources, the team could had implemented better strategies to allow player to team up or be against each others. Extended functionality, like a versus team modality and additional maps, could also be implemented, as well as a singleplayer adventure.

Finally, allowing the game to have multiple monsters could also have been a great main mechanic: while the game surely offers base game functionality, additional options like the ones just described could deepen the immersion of players.